WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220 line:-2 align:center
We introduce Proxy Policy Steering an inference time adaptation method for robotics foundation models

2
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.400 line:-2 align:center
Generalist policies carry broad manipulation priors from large scale data

3
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:15.960 line:-2 align:center
However they still struggle in downstream tasks that are underrepresented in the training distribution

4
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:20.440 line:-2 align:center
Direct fine tuning methods tackle this by modifying the

5
00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.520 line:-2 align:center
base policy weights reshaping the base policy's action distribution

6
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:27.580 line:-2 align:center
While this effectively adapts the policy behavior it can overfit

7
00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.060 line:-2 align:center
to the limited demonstrations and lose the general priors

8
00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:35.380 line:-2 align:center
Existing steering methods like DSRL change the initial noise or

9
00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:38.160 line:-2 align:center
flow matching process to select actions suitable for the task

10
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.060 line:-2 align:center
However the actions are still sampled from the

11
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:43.540 line:-2 align:center
base distribution and do not deliver novel behaviors

12
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:48.560 line:-2 align:center
How could generalist policies learn new skills without losing the base prior

13
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.940 line:-2 align:center
We propose Proxy Policy Steering

14
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.780 line:-2 align:center
Instead of reshaping the base action distribution we estimate how task supervision would change it

15
00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.820 line:-2 align:center
Specifically we distill the base policy into a lightweight reference proxy under task observations

16
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.620 line:-2 align:center
We then fine tune this proxy under task supervision to obtain a task proxy

17
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.080 line:-2 align:center
At inference we take the velocity predictions of all three

18
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:14.320 line:-2 align:center
models subtract the reference velocity and add the task velocity

19
00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:19.760 line:-2 align:center
This residual captures the task induced shift steering the

20
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.240 line:-2 align:center
policy to approximate the fine tuned action distribution

21
00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:27.440 line:-2 align:center
Facing a state where the base policy needs guidance to locate the correct

22
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.780 line:-2 align:center
grasping position the residual steers the base toward the task proxy which guides

23
00:01:32.780 --> 00:01:35.580 line:-2 align:center
the robot to perform correct grasping as a novel task behavior

24
00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:42.720 line:-2 align:center
In a state that is not covered in the task demonstrations due

25
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.900 line:-2 align:center
to a failed grasp the base policy shows generic recovery behavior

26
00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:51.600 line:-2 align:center
Here our proxy policies share the same overfitting errors making the residual near zero

27
00:01:52.300 --> 00:01:55.920 line:-2 align:center
Therefore the base retains control and recovers from the grasping failure

28
00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:04.280 line:-2 align:center
When the state aligns with both the pre training distribution and

29
00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:07.780 line:-2 align:center
the task demonstration support the three policies reach an agreement

30
00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:15.680 line:-2 align:center
By combining proxy residual with the base prior PPS injects task guidance while retaining

31
00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:20.160 line:-2 align:center
the general priors of the base model achieving robust manipulation in downstream tasks

32
00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.160 line:-2 align:center
We evaluate PPS on 12 tasks spanning long horizon

33
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.400 line:-2 align:center
goals deformable and articulated objects and diverse skills

34
00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:33.900 line:-2 align:center
PPS outperforms LoRA fine tuning previous steering methods and train from scratch policies

35
00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.460 line:-2 align:center
Since PPS keeps the base model intact it

36
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.380 line:-2 align:center
preserves the general priors from the base model

37
00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.420 line:-2 align:center
This helps the policy recover under human interventions even

38
00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:44.780 line:-2 align:center
though no recovery behavior is collected in the demonstrations

39
00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:55.780 line:-2 align:center
Whereas LoRA fine tuning can overfit to the small scale demonstrations and

40
00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.500 line:-2 align:center
overwrite parts of the manipulation priors causing it to fail under perturbations

41
00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:07.480 line:-2 align:center
PPS also enables injecting information from additional modalities into the base model such as using

42
00:03:07.480 --> 00:03:12.000 line:-2 align:center
point cloud to locate textureless objects and leveraging audio inputs to find the sound source

43
00:03:13.780 --> 00:03:14.780 line:-2 align:center
Thanks for watching
